# RULE (CLAUDE.md, "ONE LOG, ONE SPELLING"; bugs.md 47):
#   normalizeLogPath() and logSettingsKey() are IDEMPOTENT, and the whole settings
#   tree turns on it — every entry point normalizes before the string becomes a
#   Document::path() and Document::prepare() normalizes AGAIN, while
#   LogFileStore::save() re-keys an address its caller has already keyed. Both
#   clean a local path, and QDir::cleanPath() is NOT ITSELF IDEMPOTENT: dropping a
#   `.` component leaves a separator behind at a position the collapse pass has
#   already walked past, so `/.//a.zip` cleans to `//a.zip` and only a second pass
#   reaches `/a.zip`. Both funnels run cleanedToFixedPoint() over what their own
#   single clean answered, rather than taking that answer for clean
#
#   THE MUTATION IS THAT HELPER MADE THE IDENTITY, which is exactly the code as it
#   shipped: the caller cleans once and believes the result. Cutting the LOOP down
#   to a fixed count is deliberately not the mutation and is worth not
#   re-attempting — swept over every string of up to nine characters drawn from
#   `/`, `.` and `a`, one further pass settles every input there is, so no case a
#   test could name separates one extra clean from the loop. The loop is the
#   argument that a fixed count is wrong the day Qt's cleaner acquires another
#   such wrinkle, which is about a future Qt rather than about an input that
#   exists now, and a mutation nothing can kill would read as the rule being inert
#   when it is merely forward-looking
#
# GUARD: tst_archivelocation::theSecondNormalizeMovesNothingHoweverTheContainerIsSpelled
#
# SILENT WHEN BROKEN: an archived or local log spelled with a root-anchored `/.`
# opens perfectly and has two spellings — the one the open path cleaned to and the
# one Document::prepare() cleaned that into. Nothing reports it: the tab is right,
# the records are right, and what is wrong is that the log burns a second slot out
# of the pool of 500 and has its filters, highlight rules and format written under
# one name and read back under the other, so every setting it is given is
# forgotten on the next open. The address fuzz target sees it as a fixed-point
# violation; a person sees a log that will not remember anything.

diff --git a/src/core/RemoteLocation.cpp b/src/core/RemoteLocation.cpp
--- a/src/core/RemoteLocation.cpp
+++ b/src/core/RemoteLocation.cpp
@@ -417,15 +417,7 @@
 // still moves is at least a DETERMINISTIC one, which is all any caller needs.
 QString cleanedToFixedPoint(const QString &path)
 {
-    constexpr int kMaxCleanPasses = 4;
-    QString cleaned = path;
-    for (int pass = 0; pass < kMaxCleanPasses; ++pass) {
-        const QString again = QDir::cleanPath(cleaned);
-        if (again == cleaned)
-            break;
-        cleaned = again;
-    }
-    return (QDir::isAbsolutePath(path) && !QDir::isAbsolutePath(cleaned)) ? path : cleaned;
+    return path;
 }
 
 // The one composite both funnels answer a LOCAL address with: made absolute, then
